---
// /society — The Society: a mind of mindless parts, live in the browser.
// Prototype (2026-08-05). Launched unlisted — a new experiment shows itself before it
// claims a shelf place — and listed in the /holdings register on Frank's go the next
// morning (src/data/werke.ts, ranked third).
import Page from '@/layouts/Page.astro'
import SocietyFigure from '@/components/society/SocietyFigure.astro'
---

<Page
  title="The Society | Frank Bültge"
  description="A mind of mindless parts, running live in your browser — after Marvin Minsky's The Society of Mind (1986), forty years on. It perceives you; you may silence any of its agents."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-14">
    <header class="max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-muted">
        experiment · prototype · started 2026-08-05
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-2 text-3xl font-semibold">The Society</h1>
      <p class="mt-1 text-fg-muted">a mind of mindless parts</p>

      <blockquote class="mt-6 border-l-2 pl-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        “What magical trick makes us intelligent? The trick is that there is no trick. The
        power of intelligence stems from our vast diversity, not from any single, perfect
        principle.”
        <footer class="mt-1 text-xs">
          — Marvin Minsky, <cite>The Society of Mind</cite>, §30.8 (1986)
        </footer>
      </blockquote>

      <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        Forty years ago Minsky claimed a mind is a society of agents, each one mindless
        alone. Today's machine minds bet on the opposite: one vast, opaque model. This piece
        runs Minsky's architecture in your browser as a counter-model in a glass case —
        twenty-five agents, every one small enough to read whole, building block towers,
        quarrelling over one hand, remembering by K-line, watched by a B-brain that can see
        only them, never the world.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed">
        You are in it. Your pointer is a shadow in the block world: the society notices
        motion without ever knowing what moves, grows curious, gets bored of you, and
        startles if you are sudden. And you may hurt it: select any agent and silence it —
        what the whole loses is not scripted anywhere; it follows from the missing rule. A
        reload heals everything.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <SocietyFigure />
    </div>

    <section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl" aria-label="Under proof">
      <h2 class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-fg-muted">
        under proof
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-fg-muted">
        The piece's claims are tests, not captions
        (<code class="text-xs">src/lib/society/engine.test.ts</code>): the same seed is the
        same morning, tick for tick · left alone, the society finishes a tower, though no
        agent can build · an intact society wrecks only finished towers; silence
        CENSOR-WRECK and it smashes its own unfinished work · silence GRASP and no block is
        ever placed again · silence SEE-MOTION and you become invisible to it. Chapter and
        section numbers are verified against the 1986 edition; the thirty-chapter shelf
        shows honestly which parts of the book live here so far — ten chapters are awake,
        twenty are roadmap.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Page>
